WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:07.850
Yasin Aboukir earned the first place and the
most valuable hacker award at H1303 live hacking

00:00:07.850 --> 00:00:08.850
event.

00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:13.969
In this podcast, he'll describe his 10 year
long journey of how he got there.

00:00:13.969 --> 00:00:19.960
You will learn what's his methodology, how
did it change or what are the underrated hacking

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:21.250
techniques.

00:00:21.250 --> 00:00:26.010
But apart from this, he also does the hacking
while traveling the world and doing a lot

00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:31.540
of sports and basically enjoying the life
and we'll also talk about this in this episode.

00:00:31.540 --> 00:00:32.540
Enjoy.

00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:34.170
Hello, Yassine.

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:38.430
Thank you so much for joining me today for
this interview.

00:00:38.430 --> 00:00:43.469
For those viewers who don't know you, can
you please introduce yourself a little bit?

00:00:43.469 --> 00:00:44.469
All right.

00:00:44.469 --> 00:00:46.000
Thank you so much for having me.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:47.010
Appreciate it.

00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:48.719
So my name is Yassine Aboukir.

00:00:48.719 --> 00:00:50.970
Born and raised in Morocco.

00:00:50.970 --> 00:00:52.770
I'm 28 years old.

00:00:52.770 --> 00:00:54.570
I'm based in France.

00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:59.390
And I've been doing application security consulting
for a while.

00:00:59.390 --> 00:01:03.700
But I've also been doing a lot of bug bounties
on this site.

00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:09.159
So I started doing bug bounties in 2013 and
then so on.

00:01:09.159 --> 00:01:14.770
I started on HackerOne, but I've done some
bug hunting like on and off platform for a

00:01:14.770 --> 00:01:16.640
while as well.

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:19.880
So I worked for two years at HackerOne as
well.

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:27.950
I did some triage from 2017 to 2019 and then
just started doing bug bounties full time

00:01:27.950 --> 00:01:29.020
again.

00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:31.950
Also, the tragedy, I've done it part time.

00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:34.840
So I was actually doing bug bounties as well.

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:41.619
So yeah, until now, I've been to a couple
of live hacking events as well with HackerOne.

00:01:41.619 --> 00:01:44.170
It was an interesting experience.

00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:50.450
And currently, I'm a member of the Hacker
Advisory Board at HackerOne as well.

00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:53.490
And I do some pain testing with them too.

00:01:53.490 --> 00:02:02.780
So most of the things that I do are kind of
related to HackerOne. 2013 is very early for

00:02:02.780 --> 00:02:04.020
starting bug bounty.

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:05.380
How did you start?

00:02:05.380 --> 00:02:08.929
How did you get interested in it back then?

00:02:08.929 --> 00:02:11.640
Yeah, it was actually, it was the early days.

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:18.170
So basically, even before doing bug bounties,
I was doing hacking as a hobby.

00:02:18.170 --> 00:02:22.420
So I really loved like finding security bugs
on software.

00:02:22.420 --> 00:02:28.310
But back then, I had kind of a wrong idea
of how responsible disclosure worked.

00:02:28.310 --> 00:02:32.440
So basically, what I was doing, I was finding
bugs on software, and then I would just go

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:36.810
straight and post them on the exploit databases.

00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:38.250
Like back then was Milwarme.

00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:41.390
I don't know if you know the name, but now
it's like exploit database.

00:02:41.390 --> 00:02:45.319
So I just like post them there like for some
sort of clout.

00:02:45.319 --> 00:02:51.220
I mean, to raise awareness as well, but I
had a wrong idea of responsible disclosure.

00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:56.519
But then one day, I was just reading news
about cybersecurity news, and I stumbled upon

00:02:56.519 --> 00:03:02.409
an article that was talking about HackerOne
and how you could actually now start hacking

00:03:02.409 --> 00:03:04.940
legally and getting paid for that.

00:03:04.940 --> 00:03:10.430
So I signed up immediately on the platform
in 2013.

00:03:10.430 --> 00:03:15.390
But back then, there were not so many programs
like nowadays, there was just like a bunch

00:03:15.390 --> 00:03:20.860
of open source projects like Python, Django,
Ruby on Rails.

00:03:20.860 --> 00:03:25.900
I did not have like a very solid skill set
back then.

00:03:25.900 --> 00:03:30.630
So it took me a while to actually find my
very first bug.

00:03:30.630 --> 00:03:33.670
So yeah, that's how I got started on HackerOne.

00:03:33.670 --> 00:03:38.520
And for how long did you do it full time?

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:45.030
I was doing it like starting from 2013 on
this side, like in parallel with my studies,

00:03:45.030 --> 00:03:47.019
because I was still doing my studies.

00:03:47.019 --> 00:03:50.890
So I've done it until 2018.

00:03:50.890 --> 00:03:53.069
Like just on this side as a hobby.

00:03:53.069 --> 00:03:59.120
And then 2018, when I graduated, I started
doing it full time.

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:03.580
Okay, and you also worked as triageur.

00:04:03.580 --> 00:04:08.489
Yes, this look like how do you describe the
experience?

00:04:08.489 --> 00:04:12.140
Basically, I joined the triage team in 2017.

00:04:12.140 --> 00:04:17.780
After I passed the interviews, we were very
few people at the company, even at the triage

00:04:17.780 --> 00:04:23.360
team, we're just like a couple of people like
me, Patrick, now I'm sick, a few other guys

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:25.540
like Everton from Brazil.

00:04:25.540 --> 00:04:29.230
So the triage was still an early job back
then.

00:04:29.230 --> 00:04:33.990
So we had to figure out all the processes
for triage, especially for triaging during

00:04:33.990 --> 00:04:36.630
the life hacking events, because it's a live
triage.

00:04:36.630 --> 00:04:38.120
So we had to figure out all the processes.

00:04:38.120 --> 00:04:43.389
And like we were just triaging for a couple
of companies, it was just a few back then.

00:04:43.389 --> 00:04:49.500
But now we started growing, growing, and until
the team got bigger.

00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:56.050
It was an amazing experience because I had
exposure to this, the bug hunting community.

00:04:56.050 --> 00:05:00.460
I was very fortunate to be invited to a bunch
of life hacking events to triage there.

00:05:00.460 --> 00:05:03.420
So I got the chance to meet so many of the
hackers.

00:05:03.420 --> 00:05:06.110
That's where I made so many of my connections.

00:05:06.110 --> 00:05:10.740
It was just also like a great learning experience
for me as a hacker.

00:05:10.740 --> 00:05:14.419
I mean, we're not going to deny that it was
like you triage a lot of reports coming from

00:05:14.419 --> 00:05:15.740
hackers.

00:05:15.740 --> 00:05:20.100
So you get exposed to a lot of knowledge there
as well.

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:28.000
It's just dealing with the team, the programs,
the companies, like having a different perspective

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:33.169
on the bug bounty hunting, just like from
a customer side and as well from the hacker

00:05:33.169 --> 00:05:34.169
side.

00:05:34.169 --> 00:05:36.530
So it was a great, great experience for sure.

00:05:36.530 --> 00:05:39.010
And I did it for two years.

00:05:39.010 --> 00:05:41.290
But yeah.

00:05:41.290 --> 00:05:47.350
What do you think is the most important thing
that you learn as a triager that you just

00:05:47.350 --> 00:05:51.810
can't learn from reading these closed write
ups?

00:05:51.810 --> 00:06:00.960
Probably is that a lot of people, they all
have high expectations when they're submitting

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:02.669
reports.

00:06:02.669 --> 00:06:09.020
And it's just like what I've learned is that
every company has different threat modeling.

00:06:09.020 --> 00:06:15.880
So while a company may pay for a specific
class of vulnerability, another company may

00:06:15.880 --> 00:06:18.250
not be able to afford that.

00:06:18.250 --> 00:06:23.460
Or it's just because the issue is not really
as impactful as you may think.

00:06:23.460 --> 00:06:28.990
So as a bug bounty hunter, always make sure
to lower your expectations when you're doing

00:06:28.990 --> 00:06:31.210
hunting and not have high expectations.

00:06:31.210 --> 00:06:38.410
And at the end, be very disappointed because
you were not you're not getting the outcome

00:06:38.410 --> 00:06:40.419
that you were hoping for.

00:06:40.419 --> 00:06:43.850
So I think that's one of the things I learned.

00:06:43.850 --> 00:06:50.080
Okay, yeah, I would love to spend some time
working as a triager.

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:54.580
But I guess just for a month, because I don't
think I would actually like to work.

00:06:54.580 --> 00:06:57.830
I would just like to see how it looks like
from the other side.

00:06:57.830 --> 00:07:01.949
Yeah, but it is like for a month or a few
weeks.

00:07:01.949 --> 00:07:02.949
I agree.

00:07:02.949 --> 00:07:05.910
It is very, it is an interesting job for sure.

00:07:05.910 --> 00:07:08.560
But also there is a lot of burnout in it.

00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:14.520
Because like you're doing the same thing over
and over and it can be exhausting, like really,

00:07:14.520 --> 00:07:15.539
really exhausting.

00:07:15.539 --> 00:07:19.979
So it's not an easy job to do for sure.

00:07:19.979 --> 00:07:25.069
Yeah, it looks like a very, very tough job
to me.

00:07:25.069 --> 00:07:29.860
When you're hunting, what's your hacking style?

00:07:29.860 --> 00:07:30.979
My hacking style.

00:07:30.979 --> 00:07:37.729
So if you're talking about the methodology,
then in the early days of hacking, I actually

00:07:37.729 --> 00:07:40.069
I used to do a lot of reconnaissance.

00:07:40.069 --> 00:07:47.129
I do so much reconnaissance to have like a
certain data set that I can start hacking

00:07:47.129 --> 00:07:48.129
on.

00:07:48.129 --> 00:07:51.110
And then I started doing hacking manually.

00:07:51.110 --> 00:07:56.970
So basically it was like a 50-50 kind of methodology,
like some recon and then you have a data set

00:07:56.970 --> 00:07:58.740
and start hacking manually.

00:07:58.740 --> 00:08:06.979
But then like I progressively actually started
focusing a lot or just on the main application.

00:08:06.979 --> 00:08:12.210
So basically if I'm hacking on a certain application
and they have an open scope, I just focus

00:08:12.210 --> 00:08:14.050
on the main app, the main functionalities.

00:08:14.050 --> 00:08:19.080
And I go deep on understanding the app and
how it works.

00:08:19.080 --> 00:08:25.139
Just like navigating it as a regular user
and writing down everything and then trying

00:08:25.139 --> 00:08:27.830
to figure out where we can find the bugs from
there.

00:08:27.830 --> 00:08:31.530
But in the early days, I was doing a lot of
recon and then manual hacking.

00:08:31.530 --> 00:08:35.460
But now it's almost pure manual on the main
app.

00:08:35.460 --> 00:08:39.890
So that's how I like to hack.

00:08:39.890 --> 00:08:45.861
And when you open like a huge app with like
hundreds of endpoints and you know it's a

00:08:45.861 --> 00:08:52.089
public bug bounty program probably looked
at by dozens of hunters, what do you do when

00:08:52.089 --> 00:08:55.560
you start hacking on a target like this?

00:08:55.560 --> 00:08:59.690
Actually that's my favorite kind of programs.

00:08:59.690 --> 00:09:06.770
The programs that have like a complex application
with so many endpoints and so many functionalities

00:09:06.770 --> 00:09:08.730
that are all connected together.

00:09:08.730 --> 00:09:15.209
I think that's the best place where you can
find some interesting bugs, especially logical

00:09:15.209 --> 00:09:17.990
bugs like everything is just connected.

00:09:17.990 --> 00:09:23.920
When one side breaks, the other side will
break as well as a consequence.

00:09:23.920 --> 00:09:28.660
So it's my most interesting kind of program.

00:09:28.660 --> 00:09:32.519
When I started, it doesn't matter like there
were so many hackers that hacked it before

00:09:32.519 --> 00:09:35.850
because I actually think that's a good sign.

00:09:35.850 --> 00:09:41.450
It means that the program is a matcher program
because it's attracting a lot of hackers.

00:09:41.450 --> 00:09:44.260
So it has a good hacker engagement.

00:09:44.260 --> 00:09:45.380
So it's a good thing.

00:09:45.380 --> 00:09:50.040
For example, if we look at PayPal, there are
so many resolved bugs.

00:09:50.040 --> 00:09:54.190
There are so many hackers, phone bugs on PayPal.

00:09:54.190 --> 00:10:01.110
But still, I've hacked on PayPal in the past
months, I still managed to find many bugs

00:10:01.110 --> 00:10:02.110
on the application.

00:10:02.110 --> 00:10:06.610
So it doesn't really matter that there were
hackers that hacked it before because like

00:10:06.610 --> 00:10:11.870
the big programs like PayPal, for example,
they always have many code changes.

00:10:11.870 --> 00:10:17.959
They push code on a daily basis, like building
up functionalities every day.

00:10:17.959 --> 00:10:21.350
So there will always be bugs to be found.

00:10:21.350 --> 00:10:27.880
So what I personally do is just say open the
app immediately and just start intercepting

00:10:27.880 --> 00:10:34.560
traffic and just start browsing the application
as a regular user, making sure I'm very familiar

00:10:34.560 --> 00:10:42.380
with each part of the application so I can
have a better visualization of the attack

00:10:42.380 --> 00:10:43.380
surface.

00:10:43.380 --> 00:10:48.700
And then from there, I can decide which functionalities
are attracting my attention.

00:10:48.700 --> 00:10:55.830
If I get some interesting leads or something
that I think will be worth looking into.

00:10:55.830 --> 00:11:03.829
So I just start from there and hack one functionality
by the other.

00:11:03.829 --> 00:11:09.980
So I look at the functionality, I just think
what kind of vulnerabilities will be applicable

00:11:09.980 --> 00:11:11.940
to this functionality.

00:11:11.940 --> 00:11:19.779
What kind of vulnerabilities that I can test
against and I go from there.

00:11:19.779 --> 00:11:24.311
Do you prefer client-side or server-side bugs
or you just don't care, you look at functionality

00:11:24.311 --> 00:11:28.080
and you look for whatever you can find there?

00:11:28.080 --> 00:11:30.380
I do a balance of both actually.

00:11:30.380 --> 00:11:37.500
I like finding client-side bugs just as much
as I enjoy finding server-side bugs.

00:11:37.500 --> 00:11:44.930
So basically, as I mentioned, when I'm testing,
I don't just go and look for certain type

00:11:44.930 --> 00:11:47.570
of bugs or certain class of vulnerabilities.

00:11:47.570 --> 00:11:53.510
I just go functionality by functionality and
see what kind of bugs will apply to it, either

00:11:53.510 --> 00:11:55.730
client-side or server-side, it doesn't matter.

00:11:55.730 --> 00:12:01.180
I think that's been helpful and that's one
of the advantages of my methodology, I guess,

00:12:01.180 --> 00:12:03.320
so I don't miss out on other bugs.

00:12:03.320 --> 00:12:10.709
So basically, I try to focus on high severity
bugs, basically, but while hunting, I might

00:12:10.709 --> 00:12:16.670
stumble upon a medium, a low severity and
if it's worth it, I will definitely submit

00:12:16.670 --> 00:12:18.050
it as well.

00:12:18.050 --> 00:12:22.580
So just go with the mindset that you're going
to look for high severity bugs because they

00:12:22.580 --> 00:12:23.580
pay more.

00:12:23.580 --> 00:12:29.500
But then if I stumble upon any medium, I would
definitely submit them as well.

00:12:29.500 --> 00:12:36.450
Okay, and what tools do you use and if you
use Burb, please mention some Burb extensions.

00:12:36.450 --> 00:12:43.500
Yes, I mainly, as I mentioned, I used to do
a lot of recon and automation, but now that

00:12:43.500 --> 00:12:47.560
I am focusing more on the app, I mainly just
use Burb suite.

00:12:47.560 --> 00:12:54.310
So I use Burb suite and for the extensions,
I use the Turbo intruder for the race condition

00:12:54.310 --> 00:12:55.560
kind of vulnerabilities.

00:12:55.560 --> 00:12:57.070
I use Authorize.

00:12:57.070 --> 00:12:58.180
It's an amazing extension.

00:12:58.180 --> 00:13:01.720
I use it for privilege and access control
issues.

00:13:01.720 --> 00:13:04.620
It just saves you a lot of time.

00:13:04.620 --> 00:13:06.180
I use Paraminer.

00:13:06.180 --> 00:13:13.250
It's one of my favorite as well because I
love to enumerate for hidden params and headers

00:13:13.250 --> 00:13:14.250
as well.

00:13:14.250 --> 00:13:16.620
So Paraminer, I use it a lot.

00:13:16.620 --> 00:13:20.649
And what else?

00:13:20.649 --> 00:13:22.089
I think that's basically it.

00:13:22.089 --> 00:13:25.519
I use the ActiveScan++ as well sometimes.

00:13:25.519 --> 00:13:35.160
It's a good scanner and there is the extension
that paints a bunch of random headers on the

00:13:35.160 --> 00:13:36.160
request.

00:13:36.160 --> 00:13:37.160
I forgot the name.

00:13:37.160 --> 00:13:39.190
I use that one very well as well.

00:13:39.190 --> 00:13:44.800
When you're browsing, it just paints a bunch
of random headers and checks the behavior

00:13:44.800 --> 00:13:45.810
of the page.

00:13:45.810 --> 00:13:49.649
I forget the name, but I use that extension
as well.

00:13:49.649 --> 00:13:53.019
So I use InMap as well for port scanning.

00:13:53.019 --> 00:13:56.150
And there are times when I do some recon for
sure.

00:13:56.150 --> 00:14:00.519
So I would use E-MAS for subdomain enumerations.

00:14:00.519 --> 00:14:06.279
I use the tool from Corbin, GAU, to find all
the endpoints.

00:14:06.279 --> 00:14:09.170
This is actually, I love that tool.

00:14:09.170 --> 00:14:10.519
I use it most of the time.

00:14:10.519 --> 00:14:14.860
It's even if you're hacking on the main app
because you want to find all the endpoints,

00:14:14.860 --> 00:14:17.589
the API endpoints, all the other index stuff.

00:14:17.589 --> 00:14:19.300
So it helps a lot.

00:14:19.300 --> 00:14:22.170
So InMap, GAU.

00:14:22.170 --> 00:14:31.160
I use HTTPX to find the metadata, the title,
the server, the status, data like that.

00:14:31.160 --> 00:14:32.779
So yeah.

00:14:32.779 --> 00:14:37.970
And shout out to Project Discovery.

00:14:37.970 --> 00:14:40.480
They have amazing tools.

00:14:40.480 --> 00:14:43.800
I just want to give them a shout out.

00:14:43.800 --> 00:14:44.800
Yeah, true.

00:14:44.800 --> 00:14:48.550
Do you have better results now that you don't
do recon?

00:14:48.550 --> 00:14:53.680
Not better results, but actually I mean better
payouts.

00:14:53.680 --> 00:14:56.130
That's how I would put it.

00:14:56.130 --> 00:15:01.500
Before when I was doing a lot of recon, it's
just a bunch, I find a lot of medium and low

00:15:01.500 --> 00:15:03.260
CX vulnerabilities.

00:15:03.260 --> 00:15:08.940
But now that I started focusing on the main
app, I actually noticed that the payouts are

00:15:08.940 --> 00:15:11.130
far bigger.

00:15:11.130 --> 00:15:16.550
Because the companies mostly appreciate when
you hack on the main app because they're more

00:15:16.550 --> 00:15:22.350
interested in you to find bugs that affects
their customers.

00:15:22.350 --> 00:15:27.190
But then when you do a lot of recon, it's
just like a bunch of outdated assets, maybe

00:15:27.190 --> 00:15:34.880
subdomain takeovers, excesses on an interesting
subdomains where there is basically no impact.

00:15:34.880 --> 00:15:38.060
But when you focus on the main app, there
is always impact.

00:15:38.060 --> 00:15:40.930
There's always a security impact that you
know will matter to the company.

00:15:40.930 --> 00:15:48.630
So I noticed the better payouts to say the
least.

00:15:48.630 --> 00:15:56.870
Okay, what do you think is a bug class or
a technique or maybe a tool that you think

00:15:56.870 --> 00:15:59.100
people undervalue?

00:15:59.100 --> 00:16:03.790
A tool or a technique?

00:16:03.790 --> 00:16:12.270
I definitely say fuzzing, like fuzzing all
the inputs, whether it's like a header, which

00:16:12.270 --> 00:16:19.390
is like a typical input, which is like try
all kind of characters or kind of unique characters

00:16:19.390 --> 00:16:22.260
and start fuzzing for all kind of stuff.

00:16:22.260 --> 00:16:27.320
So I think fuzzing because you're pushing
the application to behave in a certain unexpected

00:16:27.320 --> 00:16:32.410
way and then they always get some sort of
information from that behavior.

00:16:32.410 --> 00:16:36.950
So fuzz all the things I would say.

00:16:36.950 --> 00:16:40.180
What wordlist do you use for this?

00:16:40.180 --> 00:16:45.180
Basically for fuzzing, I use Intruder, I use
the Turbo with a bunch of wordlists.

00:16:45.180 --> 00:16:53.380
So that will do it if you're doing the web
application hacking, of course.

00:16:53.380 --> 00:16:59.130
How often do you hack yourself and how often
do you collaborate with others?

00:16:59.130 --> 00:17:02.670
For me, hacking, I think it depends.

00:17:02.670 --> 00:17:07.720
I don't really have a fixed schedule so it
depends on if I feel like hacking.

00:17:07.720 --> 00:17:17.020
But I think I would definitely average at
least maybe 25 to 30 hours a week of hacking

00:17:17.020 --> 00:17:18.539
by myself.

00:17:18.539 --> 00:17:20.510
I do collaborate as well.

00:17:20.510 --> 00:17:23.179
It depends sometimes.

00:17:23.179 --> 00:17:28.350
There are many times when friends of mine,
people that I know, they would reach out with

00:17:28.350 --> 00:17:32.631
a certain lead, something they're working
on and they're trying to escalate it or you

00:17:32.631 --> 00:17:37.160
just want to investigate what's happening
so they would just reach out to me.

00:17:37.160 --> 00:17:42.410
Or I do the same as well when I know that
I'm working on something that is not within

00:17:42.410 --> 00:17:47.690
my skill set and I reach out to people that
I know are good at that.

00:17:47.690 --> 00:17:50.539
So I do collaborate occasionally as well.

00:17:50.539 --> 00:17:56.020
I like collaboration.

00:17:56.020 --> 00:17:58.090
You mentioned the skill set here.

00:17:58.090 --> 00:18:02.429
I saw your presentation about AWS Cognito.

00:18:02.429 --> 00:18:08.679
My question is how do you choose a subject
like AWS Cognito to do some research on?

00:18:08.679 --> 00:18:13.500
If you remember, talk me through the first
time you saw this, through how did you research

00:18:13.500 --> 00:18:18.929
what misconfigurations can happen and if you
did later try to find more instances of those

00:18:18.929 --> 00:18:20.070
misconfigurations.

00:18:20.070 --> 00:18:22.360
Yes, 100%.

00:18:22.360 --> 00:18:28.960
So basically what happened with AWS Cognito
is that when I was hacking myself, either

00:18:28.960 --> 00:18:35.610
doing penetration testing or doing back bounties,
I actually stumbled upon applications that

00:18:35.610 --> 00:18:40.620
were using AWS Cognito and myself, I was not
familiar with it.

00:18:40.620 --> 00:18:46.600
Just from observing those HTTP requests that
are being sent to the AWS, they were really

00:18:46.600 --> 00:18:47.600
weird.

00:18:47.600 --> 00:18:48.710
I couldn't understand them.

00:18:48.710 --> 00:18:55.650
It was very hard for me to hack that application
because I was not familiar with the technology.

00:18:55.650 --> 00:19:00.809
And then every time I looked up on Google,
for example, I looked up for resources, I

00:19:00.809 --> 00:19:05.650
couldn't find anything that is just straightforward
like on how you would test for it.

00:19:05.650 --> 00:19:10.140
There were some articles, there were already
a bunch of published misconfigurations, but

00:19:10.140 --> 00:19:19.700
just the lack of information is what actually
motivated me to look into it a bit more and

00:19:19.700 --> 00:19:26.020
just try to collect as much information about
the AWS Cognito, what it does, how it works,

00:19:26.020 --> 00:19:27.750
and install it myself.

00:19:27.750 --> 00:19:35.070
And that's how I started looking into different
configurations in the panel and then I started

00:19:35.070 --> 00:19:40.600
reading the literature and already found so
many articles that were talking about those

00:19:40.600 --> 00:19:43.320
and some of those misconfigurations as well.

00:19:43.320 --> 00:19:44.390
There were some examples.

00:19:44.390 --> 00:19:54.380
So, yeah, that's how I started and just gathered
everything in a nice presentation.

00:19:54.380 --> 00:19:58.070
Before I did that myself, I found so many
vulnerable instances.

00:19:58.070 --> 00:20:03.930
After that, I went back to all my back-bounty
targets that I knew were actually using AWS

00:20:03.930 --> 00:20:05.890
Cognito after I got familiar with it.

00:20:05.890 --> 00:20:12.260
And I found a bunch of other misconfigurations
as a result of that, but there was one with

00:20:12.260 --> 00:20:14.490
Flickr that I had in my presentation.

00:20:14.490 --> 00:20:16.850
That one was, I haven't talked about that
one yet.

00:20:16.850 --> 00:20:23.190
It was a great finding where he managed to
take over Flickr using a bunch of AWS misconfigurations

00:20:23.190 --> 00:20:24.230
in the app.

00:20:24.230 --> 00:20:27.340
So, it was a great example to show in my presentation.

00:20:27.340 --> 00:20:32.580
Yeah, I saw it in the recent case study in
Flickr.

00:20:32.580 --> 00:20:34.620
It was disclosed.

00:20:34.620 --> 00:20:37.349
Yeah, very nice bug.

00:20:37.349 --> 00:20:44.049
Did you try then to also contact some, let's
say, automation-based hunters to maybe find

00:20:44.049 --> 00:20:48.800
even more instances of programs using Cognito?

00:20:48.800 --> 00:20:52.190
I did myself because I had my own automation.

00:20:52.190 --> 00:20:55.870
I did run it across a bunch of back-bounty
programs.

00:20:55.870 --> 00:21:04.289
I found a few, not that many, but I think
people, they wrote a Nucleid template afterwards

00:21:04.289 --> 00:21:09.470
and they started scanning for it because I
had, after the presentation, I had so many

00:21:09.470 --> 00:21:14.490
DMs from people trying to exploit those misconfigurations.

00:21:14.490 --> 00:21:22.340
So yeah, I think people went and started looking
for it after that.

00:21:22.340 --> 00:21:27.210
I saw you got the MVH award at the recent
lifehacking event.

00:21:27.210 --> 00:21:29.380
Huge congratulations for this.

00:21:29.380 --> 00:21:33.850
My question is, did you do anything differently
this time or is it just things just clicked

00:21:33.850 --> 00:21:37.600
and you did more or less the same as usual?

00:21:37.600 --> 00:21:38.600
Thank you so much.

00:21:38.600 --> 00:21:39.600
I appreciate it.

00:21:39.600 --> 00:21:44.050
For those people who are not familiar with
the MVH, so basically HackerOne, the platform,

00:21:44.050 --> 00:21:47.920
they host a bunch of lifehacking events in
different cities in the world.

00:21:47.920 --> 00:21:52.600
It could be in the United States, it could
be in the Netherlands, it could be in Portugal.

00:21:52.600 --> 00:21:59.220
So these lifehacking events, they bring back-bounty
programs and they invite a bunch of hackers

00:21:59.220 --> 00:22:02.970
based on different criteria to hack these
programs.

00:22:02.970 --> 00:22:09.350
So I won the MVH at the end of the event,
there is a prize called the Most Valuable

00:22:09.350 --> 00:22:10.350
Hacker.

00:22:10.350 --> 00:22:15.929
It's a belt that you win for your own performance
based on other criteria.

00:22:15.929 --> 00:22:27.380
So I think for me personally, for that event,
I was just very focused on the event.

00:22:27.380 --> 00:22:33.559
What made the difference actually is me focusing
on the main app, just as I mentioned.

00:22:33.559 --> 00:22:39.120
So that event, the scope was not an open scope.

00:22:39.120 --> 00:22:40.549
It was not an open scope.

00:22:40.549 --> 00:22:44.340
We had specific web applications that we were
supposed to hack.

00:22:44.340 --> 00:22:46.140
I cannot name the company, unfortunately.

00:22:46.140 --> 00:22:52.100
But yeah, I was very focused on the main app
and I spent a lot of time understanding how

00:22:52.100 --> 00:22:54.300
that application works.

00:22:54.300 --> 00:23:00.220
And I managed to find a bunch of account takeovers
and I think that one made the difference.

00:23:00.220 --> 00:23:07.409
And also what made the difference is that
I did a lot of application-based reconnaissance

00:23:07.409 --> 00:23:09.350
instead of just doing subdomain reconnaissance.

00:23:09.350 --> 00:23:16.840
What I mean by that is just like fuzzing different
parts of the application, using it as a user

00:23:16.840 --> 00:23:23.630
and just getting a good understanding of how
the app works in entirety.

00:23:23.630 --> 00:23:29.010
So I did a lot of recon and I managed to find
so many endpoints just like in JavaScript

00:23:29.010 --> 00:23:33.600
files for example, and that allowed me to
take over the account.

00:23:33.600 --> 00:23:42.039
So I think that's what helped me win that
event for sure.

00:23:42.039 --> 00:23:46.940
How do you specifically find endpoints in
JS files?

00:23:46.940 --> 00:23:55.510
So basically, it depends on JavaScript, if
it's a bigger JavaScript, I use Link Finder.

00:23:55.510 --> 00:24:02.270
It's a Python tool by Gerben from Meta Security.

00:24:02.270 --> 00:24:07.440
It's a really good tool that helps you extract
all those endpoints from the JavaScript.

00:24:07.440 --> 00:24:13.480
But sometimes I do manually go over the JavaScript
myself so I do not miss anything.

00:24:13.480 --> 00:24:21.460
Because apart from the endpoints, it could
be hard-coded credentials, there could be

00:24:21.460 --> 00:24:27.179
some client-side bugs like postMessageExcesses
or postMessageMisconfiguration that may lead

00:24:27.179 --> 00:24:30.160
to a leak or something like that.

00:24:30.160 --> 00:24:34.880
So there are some other things that you might
find in JavaScript files apart from the endpoints.

00:24:34.880 --> 00:24:37.760
And for the endpoints, I also do like Gau,
as I mentioned earlier.

00:24:37.760 --> 00:24:39.860
Gau is a really good tool.

00:24:39.860 --> 00:24:47.750
It does find so many endpoints that I use
as a starting point to start hacking sometimes.

00:24:47.750 --> 00:24:56.120
Yeah, in the recent case study, I even saw
an example of a bug that someone just found

00:24:56.120 --> 00:24:59.690
some tokens in Gau, using Gau.

00:24:59.690 --> 00:25:04.380
And they could just take, I think, reset people's
passwords with the tokens they found because

00:25:04.380 --> 00:25:05.900
they were for some reason indexed.

00:25:05.900 --> 00:25:12.630
And I really also like Gau because it just
gives you a basic idea of what endpoints is

00:25:12.630 --> 00:25:18.919
the application using, what can you look,
what parameter names are used and so on.

00:25:18.919 --> 00:25:20.500
So I also really, really like that.

00:25:20.500 --> 00:25:24.520
Yeah, it's a very good tool.

00:25:24.520 --> 00:25:27.279
You also mentioned that you do some consulting.

00:25:27.279 --> 00:25:33.990
At the moment, what is your balance between
doing bug bounty and pentest, I believe, or

00:25:33.990 --> 00:25:34.990
consulting?

00:25:34.990 --> 00:25:38.690
I would say, actually, my bug hunting is taking
over a little bit.

00:25:38.690 --> 00:25:46.270
So I would say probably like 70% bug bounty
is 30% consulting.

00:25:46.270 --> 00:25:47.990
So I do consulting.

00:25:47.990 --> 00:25:50.400
I have some clients of mine.

00:25:50.400 --> 00:25:52.810
And I also do penetration testing with HackerOne.

00:25:52.810 --> 00:25:55.539
They do pentesting consulting as well.

00:25:55.539 --> 00:25:57.760
So we work with their own clients.

00:25:57.760 --> 00:26:04.210
So if I'm not working with my own client,
I, there are occasionally when I get a pentest

00:26:04.210 --> 00:26:10.020
signed from HackerOne, and I do it when I'm
not doing consulting, I can just focus on

00:26:10.020 --> 00:26:11.370
bug bounty.

00:26:11.370 --> 00:26:16.970
So I would say like 70% bug hunting and 30%
consulting.

00:26:16.970 --> 00:26:26.000
If you could get the best parts of bug hunting
and the best parts of penetration testing,

00:26:26.000 --> 00:26:28.780
what would such an engagement look like to
you?

00:26:28.780 --> 00:26:29.799
Huh?

00:26:29.799 --> 00:26:34.179
Well, actually, it's how HackerOne is doing
it, basically.

00:26:34.179 --> 00:26:39.780
So they're doing the traditional penetration
testing, but in a crowdsourced way.

00:26:39.780 --> 00:26:44.490
So it's like the best part of the bug bounty
is, which is like you're bringing different

00:26:44.490 --> 00:26:51.360
skill set, different hacking methodologies
to the table.

00:26:51.360 --> 00:26:55.640
And you also, it's a cost effective solution
for companies.

00:26:55.640 --> 00:27:01.520
And you bring in the traditional part of the
pentesting, which is like, you're not as a

00:27:01.520 --> 00:27:05.970
bug hunter, you're only focusing on finding
high in crates, but you can also find some

00:27:05.970 --> 00:27:11.480
informatives and low severity bugs, and just
point out different areas where the company

00:27:11.480 --> 00:27:13.919
could do better or could secure itself.

00:27:13.919 --> 00:27:16.580
So it doesn't necessarily have to be just
finding criticals.

00:27:16.580 --> 00:27:18.309
Yeah, so I think...

00:27:18.309 --> 00:27:20.460
How does it specifically work?

00:27:20.460 --> 00:27:22.880
I'm not familiar.

00:27:22.880 --> 00:27:28.860
So basically, the HackerOne, they are doing
the scoping with the client, so it's like

00:27:28.860 --> 00:27:35.420
setting the scope and setting the restrictions
and everything before the engagement.

00:27:35.420 --> 00:27:42.309
And then for each pentest, there's like a
community of penetration testers working for

00:27:42.309 --> 00:27:43.309
HackerOne.

00:27:43.309 --> 00:27:52.399
So they basically assign the pentest to specific
hackers, depending on either the geographic

00:27:52.399 --> 00:27:57.950
location, or the skill set, for example, if
it's a network pentest and we've got hackers

00:27:57.950 --> 00:28:02.970
who are very good at network, they've got
relevant certifications, they might be assigned

00:28:02.970 --> 00:28:03.970
to them.

00:28:03.970 --> 00:28:07.820
So it could be based on the skill set, as
I mentioned as well, and also the availability,

00:28:07.820 --> 00:28:10.340
if the hackers are available or not.

00:28:10.340 --> 00:28:15.470
So there could be like a team of three hackers
working on a pentest with a lead, and someone

00:28:15.470 --> 00:28:17.090
is leading the pentest.

00:28:17.090 --> 00:28:23.120
So basically, you're given all the relevant
information that you need before you start,

00:28:23.120 --> 00:28:29.340
and then you just start finding bugs and also
providing updates for the company as well

00:28:29.340 --> 00:28:31.280
as for the whole team.

00:28:31.280 --> 00:28:36.220
So it's just like a traditional pentest, where
you're just bringing different...

00:28:36.220 --> 00:28:40.980
You're bringing people from different backgrounds
and different skill set.

00:28:40.980 --> 00:28:44.470
And there is not pressure on the company.

00:28:44.470 --> 00:28:47.000
Yeah, it's very interesting.

00:28:47.000 --> 00:28:51.830
I heard something about HackerOne Pentest,
but I didn't really get into how it works.

00:28:51.830 --> 00:28:57.620
Do you know how people can get involved in
doing this if they want?

00:28:57.620 --> 00:29:01.200
I would say it's more or less a new offering
for them.

00:29:01.200 --> 00:29:04.019
They're still working on scaling it.

00:29:04.019 --> 00:29:06.730
So basically, now it's an exclusive community.

00:29:06.730 --> 00:29:14.690
I think a lot of people were on board based
on their skill set, based on if they had an

00:29:14.690 --> 00:29:15.750
experience before.

00:29:15.750 --> 00:29:19.230
I think at one point, they were taking in
applications on the HackerOne platform.

00:29:19.230 --> 00:29:24.970
If you go to your profile, I think there is
a form where you can fill it out with your

00:29:24.970 --> 00:29:28.220
information and then you can apply to join
the community.

00:29:28.220 --> 00:29:35.510
But I think they might have paused it for
a while as they try to onboard more customers

00:29:35.510 --> 00:29:40.930
as well as figure out how to actually scale
it more.

00:29:40.930 --> 00:29:49.570
It still requires a bit of manual work from
their part, so it's not really a fully automated

00:29:49.570 --> 00:29:52.570
system yet.

00:29:52.570 --> 00:29:59.279
If you were to, from now on, only do bug bounty
or penetration tests, what would you choose?

00:29:59.279 --> 00:30:03.019
I would 100% do bug bounties.

00:30:03.019 --> 00:30:06.419
I started doing bug bounties a long time ago,
so it doesn't really bother me.

00:30:06.419 --> 00:30:11.980
I know there are pros and cons for each of
those, but I think I would probably be better

00:30:11.980 --> 00:30:17.380
off doing bug bounties.

00:30:17.380 --> 00:30:22.110
From a revenue standpoint, for example, I
think I'd make more doing bug bounties for

00:30:22.110 --> 00:30:23.110
sure.

00:30:23.110 --> 00:30:30.740
You just need to know what and where to focus
your time and effort on.

00:30:30.740 --> 00:30:31.740
Consulting is good as well.

00:30:31.740 --> 00:30:40.570
I mean, I would not say no, but I like the
thrill of bug bounties.

00:30:40.570 --> 00:30:42.931
I think I like taking a bit of a risk.

00:30:42.931 --> 00:30:48.159
I know bug bounty is not a very stable profession,
but I'm fine with that.

00:30:48.159 --> 00:30:51.580
The more risk, the more reward.

00:30:51.580 --> 00:30:54.789
It actually works quite well.

00:30:54.789 --> 00:31:03.740
I've been listening to some podcasts about
unpredictable rewards that you should not

00:31:03.740 --> 00:31:06.340
reward yourself every single time when you
do some good work.

00:31:06.340 --> 00:31:09.820
It should be somewhat random that this is
a good way to do it.

00:31:09.820 --> 00:31:14.790
And bug bounty is exactly this, because you
can put in the good work and not get a reward,

00:31:14.790 --> 00:31:16.950
and another time you will get a reward.

00:31:16.950 --> 00:31:24.789
So it works really well when it comes to this.

00:31:24.789 --> 00:31:29.710
I think if you can combine both, that's a
win.

00:31:29.710 --> 00:31:36.110
At least if you're having a dry month, as
we would say, you're not finding any bugs,

00:31:36.110 --> 00:31:38.580
at least you have a revenue on the side.

00:31:38.580 --> 00:31:44.000
So I think it depends on your personal circumstance
and situation.

00:31:44.000 --> 00:31:52.139
For me, for example, I don't have people depending
on me, so I would be fine having a dry month

00:31:52.139 --> 00:31:57.960
I think that would not be the end of the world
as long as you have a good foundation and

00:31:57.960 --> 00:31:59.649
good base.

00:31:59.649 --> 00:32:04.549
Yeah, that's actually my very next question.

00:32:04.549 --> 00:32:11.059
For what type of people do you think the best
would be doing bug bounty full-time?

00:32:11.059 --> 00:32:15.490
For what people it would be best to do freelance
pentesting?

00:32:15.490 --> 00:32:20.649
And for what people it's just better to stay
at a 9-to-5 job?

00:32:20.649 --> 00:32:27.610
So freelance pentesting, 9-to-5 job, and what's
the third one?

00:32:27.610 --> 00:32:28.610
And bug bounty.

00:32:28.610 --> 00:32:29.820
Oh, bug bounty.

00:32:29.820 --> 00:32:35.850
I think for bug bounties, probably if you're
just like starting out in your career, I think

00:32:35.850 --> 00:32:44.210
if you do bug bounties, it will be very beneficial
for you because you will build, you will have

00:32:44.210 --> 00:32:51.100
a good experience, you will have relevant
experience or achievements that you can showcase

00:32:51.100 --> 00:32:53.850
to land interviews or get a job.

00:32:53.850 --> 00:32:59.600
Also it will give you some money on the side
to do whatever you want, like finance your

00:32:59.600 --> 00:33:02.160
studies or do whatever.

00:33:02.160 --> 00:33:09.070
So if you're starting out, I think bug bounties
can be amazing to do it on the side.

00:33:09.070 --> 00:33:16.770
So even, but like once, and just as I mentioned,
it depends on your situation as well.

00:33:16.770 --> 00:33:22.470
So if you're already into your career as a
penetration tester, for example, and for example

00:33:22.470 --> 00:33:29.500
you have people depending on you, either you're
married or have kids, I don't think bug bounty

00:33:29.500 --> 00:33:33.019
could be the best route as a starting point.

00:33:33.019 --> 00:33:42.390
I think you might be better off if you actually
do some full-time job on the side and do bug

00:33:42.390 --> 00:33:43.660
bounties when you have some spare time.

00:33:43.660 --> 00:33:47.130
I think that would be a good way.

00:33:47.130 --> 00:33:48.320
But it also depends.

00:33:48.320 --> 00:33:55.360
I mean, there are people who are just resilient,
like I mean, they can take the risk, they

00:33:55.360 --> 00:33:56.360
don't really mind.

00:33:56.360 --> 00:34:00.840
Because like in bug bounties, you could have
like two months without finding anything,

00:34:00.840 --> 00:34:06.190
you do not get paid, but then the third month
you just find some crazy bug and it will just

00:34:06.190 --> 00:34:10.700
compensate for the two months that you feel
like you wasted.

00:34:10.700 --> 00:34:15.310
But then it depends on how you perceive it.

00:34:15.310 --> 00:34:20.599
Some people just like to do minimal work and
just get paid high rewards.

00:34:20.599 --> 00:34:27.240
Some people, I don't know how to explain it,
but I mean it depends on your situation.

00:34:27.240 --> 00:34:31.659
But just as I mentioned briefly, if you're
starting out, I think bug bounties could help

00:34:31.659 --> 00:34:32.659
you a lot.

00:34:32.659 --> 00:34:37.190
It helped me personally find my first job,
like with HackerOne for example.

00:34:37.190 --> 00:34:42.510
But if you're already into your career, I
think it's better off if you just do a full-time

00:34:42.510 --> 00:34:44.579
job and do bug bounties on this side.

00:34:44.579 --> 00:34:46.440
I think that will be a lot better.

00:34:46.440 --> 00:34:48.679
Does that make sense?

00:34:48.679 --> 00:34:50.909
I agree with the...

00:34:50.909 --> 00:34:52.590
Yeah, yeah, 100%.

00:34:52.590 --> 00:35:00.260
It actually relates a lot to me because when
I was leaving my job, I knew that if I wait

00:35:00.260 --> 00:35:06.910
too long and I get to like senior position
and good salary, I will always compare my

00:35:06.910 --> 00:35:11.780
earnings after leaving the job to the senior
level salary and this is harder.

00:35:11.780 --> 00:35:19.720
And also with years coming, with years I probably
will have more responsibility on my head,

00:35:19.720 --> 00:35:25.160
having people counting on my salary and it
becomes, even though with more experience,

00:35:25.160 --> 00:35:30.870
it's more logical to quit job, the better
situation is in the beginning when you're

00:35:30.870 --> 00:35:35.329
just, as you said, you are free, you have
no responsibilities and also you just don't

00:35:35.329 --> 00:35:36.960
have the...

00:35:36.960 --> 00:35:41.710
You're not as accommodated in the corporate
world, for example.

00:35:41.710 --> 00:35:42.920
Yeah, exactly.

00:35:42.920 --> 00:35:46.120
And for me personally, I really value my freedom.

00:35:46.120 --> 00:35:50.200
I value the flexibility that I have and I
put it above everything.

00:35:50.200 --> 00:35:54.890
So just being self-employed works for me very
well.

00:35:54.890 --> 00:35:57.730
So it depends on your aspirations as well.

00:35:57.730 --> 00:36:06.140
Like if you want to go like climb the corporate
ladder as they say, I mean, good luck to you

00:36:06.140 --> 00:36:08.520
but personally, I have different goals.

00:36:08.520 --> 00:36:13.650
And I don't think being in the corporate world
is going to make it happen.

00:36:13.650 --> 00:36:17.490
So it depends if it works for you, it just
works.

00:36:17.490 --> 00:36:20.950
Yeah, yeah, 100%.

00:36:20.950 --> 00:36:26.069
Also another thing that I've learned is it's
not like the next level, it's just something

00:36:26.069 --> 00:36:31.250
different for people with different skill
sets and different values in life.

00:36:31.250 --> 00:36:37.060
Like if you value independence, then obviously
you will not be happy in the corporate world.

00:36:37.060 --> 00:36:45.079
But if you value stability, predictability
and safety, then obviously you shouldn't look

00:36:45.079 --> 00:36:48.810
to people that, oh no, he's working, he's
self-employed, I should be as well.

00:36:48.810 --> 00:36:52.160
No, it's your values, so you should do what's
best for you.

00:36:52.160 --> 00:36:53.160
100%.

00:36:53.160 --> 00:36:57.760
We'll speak a bit more about the free time
and everything.

00:36:57.760 --> 00:37:03.869
Just a last question about the consulting
or penetration testing.

00:37:03.869 --> 00:37:10.480
How do you find clients outside of HackerOne
Pen Testing?

00:37:10.480 --> 00:37:17.960
So outside, I think when I first started out,
I think it was just me leveraging my own network.

00:37:17.960 --> 00:37:24.300
When I worked for HackerOne, I was very fortunate
to have met so many hackers just being on

00:37:24.300 --> 00:37:26.109
the live hacking events and such.

00:37:26.109 --> 00:37:29.550
So these hackers, they work in different companies.

00:37:29.550 --> 00:37:35.010
So when I was starting out in consulting,
I would reach out to them and they would actually,

00:37:35.010 --> 00:37:41.040
a few of them responded positively and I would
land my first few gigs.

00:37:41.040 --> 00:37:48.329
So basically I leveraged my network and then
I had a bunch of gigs and engagements that

00:37:48.329 --> 00:37:50.030
came in through LinkedIn.

00:37:50.030 --> 00:37:51.670
So I use LinkedIn as well.

00:37:51.670 --> 00:37:56.340
Although now I don't really personally do
any cold reach outs myself.

00:37:56.340 --> 00:38:02.570
I just built a network that I leverage to
find those gigs.

00:38:02.570 --> 00:38:07.780
So I do have some offers that come from LinkedIn
as well.

00:38:07.780 --> 00:38:09.520
I have success with back bounties as well.

00:38:09.520 --> 00:38:14.880
There was a program, for example, that I hacked
a lot and they were really satisfied with

00:38:14.880 --> 00:38:15.880
my results.

00:38:15.880 --> 00:38:21.430
So they reached out to me to sign a contract
with them and do pen testing for them.

00:38:21.430 --> 00:38:24.180
So that helps as well, doing back bounties.

00:38:24.180 --> 00:38:27.339
I think it's a good way to meet people.

00:38:27.339 --> 00:38:33.010
Just putting out myself out there, just from
social media, as they say, LinkedIn or just

00:38:33.010 --> 00:38:37.420
on Twitter, for example, it's definitely been
helping expand my network.

00:38:37.420 --> 00:38:41.839
We're just speaking at different conferences
and going there and meeting people.

00:38:41.839 --> 00:38:45.970
So you put out your name out there and people
will definitely reach out to you if you've

00:38:45.970 --> 00:38:50.410
got the right skill set that they want.

00:38:50.410 --> 00:38:59.580
Any tips for people for building a good network
of people that they know and they can rely

00:38:59.580 --> 00:39:02.359
on to contact them?

00:39:02.359 --> 00:39:07.370
So 100% networking is important for tips for
people.

00:39:07.370 --> 00:39:11.700
I would just say if you're a bug hunter, for
example, the first thing that you want to

00:39:11.700 --> 00:39:14.329
do is join those online communities.

00:39:14.329 --> 00:39:17.340
There is, for example, the HackerOne Discord
community.

00:39:17.340 --> 00:39:22.440
You're going to meet so many people chatting
there every day, just exchanging knowledge

00:39:22.440 --> 00:39:24.780
and helping each other.

00:39:24.780 --> 00:39:28.849
There is the NAMSIC Discord as well.

00:39:28.849 --> 00:39:32.700
There is a bunch of Slack channels that if
you just Google them, you're going to find

00:39:32.700 --> 00:39:33.700
them.

00:39:33.700 --> 00:39:36.530
So those are online communities that you're
going to meet like-minded people.

00:39:36.530 --> 00:39:41.640
So just starting building contacts there,
chatting with people, and following them on

00:39:41.640 --> 00:39:44.630
Twitter or maybe on LinkedIn.

00:39:44.630 --> 00:39:53.880
Just make sure you give importance to some
social media networks like LinkedIn.

00:39:53.880 --> 00:39:59.040
As bad as it looks for some people, it's actually
important to build your profile there and

00:39:59.040 --> 00:40:03.200
follow relevant people in the industry and
just putting yourself out there.

00:40:03.200 --> 00:40:09.220
I mean, that will bring you opportunities
for sure.

00:40:09.220 --> 00:40:14.880
So as we mentioned, online communities, social
media, and just going to conferences.

00:40:14.880 --> 00:40:20.160
If you go to conferences, I think I'm 100%
sure you will meet so many people.

00:40:20.160 --> 00:40:24.160
You will not only just gain knowledge from
those talks, but you will also meet people

00:40:24.160 --> 00:40:25.660
in those conferences.

00:40:25.660 --> 00:40:33.200
As long as you keep an open mind and just
always try to take the initiative, talk to

00:40:33.200 --> 00:40:34.200
people.

00:40:34.200 --> 00:40:39.690
If you go to a conference, let the people
know on Twitter that you're there if someone

00:40:39.690 --> 00:40:41.670
wants to meet up.

00:40:41.670 --> 00:40:44.869
So those are ways that you can actually expand
your network.

00:40:44.869 --> 00:40:46.940
It's not really rocket science or anything.

00:40:46.940 --> 00:40:53.550
It's just like putting yourself out there
and getting out of comfort zone to meet people.

00:40:53.550 --> 00:40:54.550
That's it.

00:40:54.550 --> 00:40:59.230
And these people like- What are your favorite
conferences?

00:40:59.230 --> 00:41:08.250
That would be definitely DEF CON in Vegas,
but there is also the CCC in Germany.

00:41:08.250 --> 00:41:12.710
CCC is a hacking convention that takes place
in Germany.

00:41:12.710 --> 00:41:18.510
I think when I was there in 2018 in Leipzig,
it was a pure hacker convention.

00:41:18.510 --> 00:41:20.950
No corporate or commercial bullshit there.

00:41:20.950 --> 00:41:23.940
Just like pure hackers doing their stuff,
creative stuff.

00:41:23.940 --> 00:41:25.569
It was an amazing conference.

00:41:25.569 --> 00:41:31.990
So I would say DEF CON in Vegas and the CCC
in Germany.

00:41:31.990 --> 00:41:35.800
And there's also my new favorite one is B-Sides
Ahmedabed.

00:41:35.800 --> 00:41:37.780
So I was there last year.

00:41:37.780 --> 00:41:39.620
It was insane.

00:41:39.620 --> 00:41:41.390
It was a huge audience, a lot of people.

00:41:41.390 --> 00:41:47.579
They were just like seeking knowledge, very
curious and just wanting to meet up.

00:41:47.579 --> 00:41:48.990
So it was a really big conference.

00:41:48.990 --> 00:41:50.970
I really enjoyed it.

00:41:50.970 --> 00:41:57.820
And the people that were speaking there, they
had really good knowledge, things to present

00:41:57.820 --> 00:41:58.920
to the world.

00:41:58.920 --> 00:42:01.680
So B-Sides Ahmedabed is one of my favorite
now.

00:42:01.680 --> 00:42:06.190
I think I might be there this year again.

00:42:06.190 --> 00:42:12.130
This is where you had the talk titled Mindset
of a Top-Tier Bug Bounty Hunter.

00:42:12.130 --> 00:42:13.410
Is it this one?

00:42:13.410 --> 00:42:14.410
Exactly.

00:42:14.410 --> 00:42:20.140
Shout out to B-Sides now because I shared
it in the newsletter and I didn't write that

00:42:20.140 --> 00:42:23.740
it was on the B-Sides conference in the newsletter.

00:42:23.740 --> 00:42:25.870
And they wrote me on Twitter, can I add this
